/*
 * LC 41 — First Missing Positive
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given unsorted integer array, return smallest missing positive integer.
 * Must run in O(n) time and O(1) space.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 1 <= nums.length <= 10^5
 * - -2^31 <= nums[i] <= 2^31 - 1
 * - O(n) TC, O(1) SC — no external data structures allowed
 *
 * KEY INSIGHT 1 — ANSWER RANGE:
 * First missing positive always in range [1, n+1].
 * With n elements, can fill at most n consecutive positives from 1.
 * Best case: array = [1,2,3...n] → answer = n+1
 * Otherwise: gap exists within [1,n] → answer <= n
 * Numbers outside [1,n] — negatives, zeros, >n — irrelevant to answer.
 *
 * KEY INSIGHT 2 — ARRAY AS HASH MAP:
 * O(1) SC means no HashSet or extra array.
 * Use input array itself as hash map:
 *   number k should sit at index k-1
 * After rearranging — first index i where nums[i] != i+1 → answer = i+1
 *
 * SWAP CONDITION:
 * while(nums[i] > 0           → only positive numbers matter
 *    && nums[i] <= n          → only numbers in valid range [1,n]
 *    && nums[nums[i]-1] != nums[i]) → avoid infinite swap on duplicates
 *
 * WHY WHILE not IF:
 * After swap, new number at index i may also need to move.
 * Keep swapping until current number is in correct place or invalid.
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach         │ Idea                             │ TC       │ SC   │
 * ├──────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force      │ Check 1,2,3... with HashSet      │ O(n)     │ O(n) │
 * │ Index as Hash    │ Rearrange in-place, scan         │ O(n)     │ O(1) │
 * └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE (HashSet)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Simple — store all numbers in HashSet, check 1,2,3... until missing.
 * HOW: Add all nums to HashSet → iterate 1 to n+1 → return first not in set.
 *
 * TC: O(n) — HashSet build + scan
 * SC: O(n) — HashSet stores all elements
 * REJECTED: Violates O(1) space requirement
 */
public int firstMissingPositiveBrute(int[] nums) {
    Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
    for(int num : nums) set.add(num);
    for(int i = 1; i <= nums.length + 1; i++)
        if(!set.contains(i)) return i;
    return nums.length + 1;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — INDEX AS HASH (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY:
 * Array itself used as hash map — O(1) extra space.
 * number k → correct position is index k-1.
 * After rearranging, scan for first mismatch.
 *
 * HOW — THREE STEPS:
 * Step 1 — rearrange: place each num at correct index via swapping
 * Step 2 — scan: first index where nums[i] != i+1 → return i+1
 * Step 3 — all present: return n+1
 *
 * SWAP CONDITION EXPLAINED:
 * nums[i] > 0        → negatives/zero can't go to valid index
 * nums[i] <= n       → numbers > n have no valid index in array
 * nums[nums[i]-1] != nums[i] → if correct position already has nums[i]
 *                               → duplicate → stop to avoid infinite loop
 *
 * TC ANALYSIS:
 * Each number moved to correct position at most once → O(n) total swaps
 * Even though nested while inside for → amortized O(n) overall
 *
 * Dry run — nums=[3,4,-1,1], n=4:
 * i=0: nums[0]=3, correct=2, nums[2]=-1≠3 → swap → [-1,4,3,1]? 
 *      wait nums[0] now=-1 > 0? NO → stop
 *      actually after swap: nums=[3→goes to idx2, idx0 gets -1]
 *      [-1,4,3,1] → nums[0]=-1 → stop
 * i=1: nums[1]=4, correct=3, nums[3]=1≠4 → swap → [-1,1,3,4]
 *      nums[1]=1, correct=0, nums[0]=-1≠1 → swap → [1,-1,3,4]
 *      nums[1]=-1 → stop
 * i=2: nums[2]=3, correct=2, nums[2]=3==3 → already correct → stop
 * i=3: nums[3]=4, correct=3, nums[3]=4==4 → already correct → stop
 *
 * Final array: [1,-1,3,4]
 * Scan:
 *   i=0: nums[0]=1 == 1 ✅
 *   i=1: nums[1]=-1 ≠ 2 → return 2 ✅
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - [1] → rearranged=[1] → all match → return 2=n+1 ✅
 * - [2,1] → rearranged=[1,2] → return 3 ✅
 * - All negatives [-1,-2] → no swaps → nums[0]≠1 → return 1 ✅
 * - Duplicates [1,1] → swap condition prevents infinite loop ✅
 *
 * TC: O(n) — each element placed at most once → amortized O(n)
 * SC: O(1) — only temp variable, no extra DS
 */
public int firstMissingPositive(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    // step 1 — place each number at correct index
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
        while(nums[i] > 0 && nums[i] <= n && nums[nums[i]-1] != nums[i]){
            // swap nums[i] to its correct position
            int correct = nums[i] - 1;
            int temp = nums[correct];
            nums[correct] = nums[i];
            nums[i] = temp;
        }
    }
    // step 2 — find first mismatch
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
        if(nums[i] != i + 1)
            return i + 1;
    }
    // step 3 — all 1 to n present
    return n + 1;
}
